ry it had become "an urban center of considerable proportions" <br>Estimated hectare size early phase III:<br>"by 450 C.E., the settlement had expanded to at least 25 hectares (over 60 acres)."<br>Estimate size at height phase III/phase IV:<br>"The total surface area of Jenne-jeno and its satellites was 69 hectares; the total population when most densely occupied approached 27,000."<br>"At its most densely populated (around AD 800) Jenne-jeno housed up to 27,000 people.<br>33 hectares. 9 hectare Hambarketolo connects to Jenne-jeno via an earthern dike.  this maximum area extent by 900-1000 CE<br>"During this time, the settlement continued to grow, reaching its maximum area of 33 hectares by 850 C.E. We know that this is so because sherds of the distinctive painted pottery that was produced at Jenne-jeno only between 450-850 C.E. are present in all our excavation units, even those near the edge of the mound. And we find them at the neighboring mound of Hambarketolo, too, suggesting that these two connected sites totaling 41 hectares (100 acres) functioned as part of a single town complex (Pl. 4). <br>\n                    '}], {'usage.total_tokens': 1412, 'usage.prompt_tokens': 1332})
