n-chief coded as: level 1, and work down to the private. Even in primitive societies such as simple chiefdoms it is often possible to distinguish at least two levels – a commander and soldiers. A complex chiefdom would be coded three levels. The presence of warrior burials might be the basis for inferring the existence of a military organization. (The lowest military level is always the individual soldier).\n                    '}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "Using the definition of 'Military Level' as a measure of hierarchical complexity within a military structure, identify the range of military levels present in the polity <polity> between <time-start> and <time-end>. Your response should reflect the levels of hierarchy from the commander-in-chief to the individual soldier and should be expressed as a JSON array in the format: [<start_of_range>, <end_of_range>]."}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '\n                        Variable name: polity territory\n                        Variable unit: square kilometres\n                        Variable description: Talking about Social Scale, Polity territory is coded in squared kilometers.\n                    '}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "Based on the concept of 'Polity Territory' as defined in terms of the area a polity controlled in squared kilometers, provide the range of the area <polity> governed from <time-start> to <time-end>. Please provide your answer in the format: [<start_of_range>, <end_of_range>], without the unit."}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '\n                        Variable name: population of the largest settlement\n                        Variable unit: Not provided\n                        Variable description:  Inhabitants.<br>"At its most densely populated (around AD 800) Jenne-jeno housed up to 27,000 people.<br>Estimate hectare size phase II:<br>settlement size "possibly exceeding 10 hectares" <br>1977 archaeological investigation established the 3rd century BCE date and showed that by the eighth-ninth century it had become "an urban center of considerable proportions" <br>Estimated hectare size early phase III:<br>"by 450 C.E., the settlement had expanded to at least 25 hectares (over 60 acres)."<br>Estimate size at height phase III/phase IV:<br>"The total surface area of Jenne-jeno and its satellites was 69 hectares; the total population when most densely occupied approached 27,000."<br>"At its most densely populated (around AD 800) Jenne-jeno housed up to 27,000 people.<br>33 hectares. 9 hectare Hambarketolo connects to Jenne-jeno via an earthern dike.  this maximum area extent by 900-1000 CE<br>"During this time, the settlement continued to grow, reaching its maximum area of 33 hectares by 850 C.E. We know that this is so because sherds of the distinctive painted pottery that was produced at Jenne-jeno only between 450-850 C.E. are present in all our excavation units, even those near the edge of the mound. And we find them at the neighboring mound of Hambarketolo, too, suggesting that these two connected sites totaling 41 hectares (100 acres) functioned as part of a single town complex (Pl. 4). <br>Estimated size phase IV:<br>12th-14th centuries population at Jenne-jeno collapsed. <br>Other notes:<br>modern town of Jenne (to be distinguished from ancient Jenne-jeno) was occupied by 500 CE. <br>"In the ninth century, two noticeable changes occur (Pl. 5)\xa0: tauf house foundations are replaced by cylindrical brick architecture, and painted pottery is replaced by pottery with impressed and stamped decoration. The source of these novelties is unknown, although we can say that they did not involve any fundamental shift in the form or general layout of either houses or pottery. So it is unlikely that any major change in the ethnic composition of Jenne-jeno was associated with the changes. Change with continuity was the prevailing pattern." <br>50,000: 1000 CE<br>"An estimate of 50,000 persons seems a reasonalbe minimum estimate for the population here at the turn of the present millennium."<br>"By the end of the millennium, fully 69 satellites are in orbit about Jenne-jeno - and this number records only the tells (permanent settlements). Sadly, we too suffer from the classic Mesopotamian disease of seriously underrepresenting the affiliated, mobile segments of the community who seasonally set up temporary (and virtually invisible) camps in the vicinity. Under-counting the nomadic populations is just the beginning of our demographic difficulties."<br>\n                    '}], {'usage.total_tokens': 1718, 'usage.prompt_tokens': 1637})
