ays about 600,000.  300,000 and 250,000 in 200 BCE and 100 CE according to the Chase-Dunn spreadsheet. <br>Alexandria: "Diodorus - who flourished in the mid- and latter part of the first century B.C./early first century A.D. and who claimed to be relying on official information - estimated the free population of Alexandria was 300,000 (17.52.6). It is not clear if this number included women and children. Strabo, who lived at the end of the first century B.C./early first century A.D., would appear to suggest a figure of approximately 500,000-600,000."<br>Ptolemaic Egypt had three big cities. The largest one is Alexandria on which the data above applies. The numbers for both Memphis and Ptolemais is between 50-100K.<br>In 60 BCE Polybius said Alexandria was the most populous city in the world. His estimate was 300K "free population". Recent estimates put the population near 1 million people  and 300K in 200 BCE . The figure of 300K does not include slaves.\n                    '}], {'usage.total_tokens': 1420, 'usage.prompt_tokens': 1344})
